%S A399071 1,6,19,25,44,333,710,103993,312689,1980127,2292816,4272943,10838702,
%T A399071 80143857,411557987,2549491779,14885392687,42106686282,1783366216531,
%U A399071 8958937768937,288469374822515,856449186698608,5706674932067741,12269799050834090,30246273033735921
%N A399071 Smallest positive integers k that minimize the Euclidean distance from point P_i to P_(i+k), where P_i = (i*cos(i), i*sin(i)), for integers i >= 1, after removing consecutive duplicates.
%C A399071 Let P_i denote points with polar coordinates (r, theta) = (i, i). Thus, all P_i lie on the Archimedean spiral r = theta. q(i) is the smallest positive integer k such that the Euclidean distance between P_i and P_(i+k) is minimized. The sequence a(n) is obtained from the sequence q(1), q(2), ... by removing consecutive duplicates.
%C A399071 The values q(i) are analogous to parastichy numbers in phyllotaxis; see the linked illustration. If the polar coordinates are changed to (r, theta) = (i, i*phi), where phi = Pi*(3 - sqrt(5)) is the golden angle, the resulting sequence begins 1, 3, 5, 8, 13, 21, ... (Fibonacci numbers; cf. A000045).
%H A399071 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399071/b399071.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..200</a>
%H A399071 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399071/a399071_2.png">Archimedean Spiral Colored by Nearest-Forward Stride q(i), 1 <= i <= 500</a>
%H A399071 Eddie Lin Rui, <a href="/A399071/a399071.py.txt">Python program for direct numerical enumeration</a>
%H A399071 Yoshikazu Yamagishi and Takamichi Sushida, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1088/1751-8121/aa9ada">Archimedean Voronoi spiral tilings</a>, Journal of Physics A: Mathematical and Theoretical, 51 (2018), 045203.
%F A399071 Let d(i,k) = ||P_(i+k) - P_i||_2. Then d(i,k)^2 = k^2 + 2*i*(i+k)*(1 - cos(k)). q(i) = min{k >= 1 : d(i,k)^2 = min_{j >= 1} d(i,j)^2}. a(n) = q(i_n), where i_1 = 1 and i_(n+1) = min{i > i_n : q(i) != q(i_n)} for n >= 1.
%e A399071 For i = 2, d(2,1)^2 ~= 6.516 and d(2,2)^2 ~= 26.658. For k >= 3, d(2,k)^2 >= k^2 >= 9 > 6.516. Hence q(2) = 1. The same enumeration gives q(1) = ... = q(6) = 1.
%e A399071 For i = 7, d(7,6)^2 ~= 43.249, which is smaller than d(7,k)^2 for 1 <= k <= 5. For k >= 7, d(7,k)^2 >= k^2 >= 49 > 43.249. Hence q(7) = 6. Thus i_1 = 1 and i_2 = 7, giving a(1) = q(i_1) = q(1) = 1 and a(2) = q(i_2) = q(7) = 6.
%Y A399071 Cf. A000045.
%Y A399071 Subsequence of A046955.
